00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:15.000
“When you are lonely, Mawusi, look for a gang. We are stronger together.”

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:23.000
Nunyati had told me London was a city of travellers. A place where you could always find one of your kind.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:38.000
I looked around, then I noticed a large group of birds perched on the steps of the square. They seemed to know their way around and they were singing.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:48.000
“Sing, Mawusi. Wherever you are, singing will bring you closer to home.”

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:55.000
A grey dove was leading the choir, inviting passers-by to join in. I went closer.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.000
“A migrant!” Shouted a blackbird, and they all turned towards me.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:04.000
It was good not to feel invisible anymore.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.000
But then, I noticed the song,

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.000
“Migrants go home. Migrants go home.”

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:19.000
They were chanting, over and over, louder and louder. When they saw me, their excitement grew.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:24.000
Next to the choir, one large pigeon was addressing the crowd from a pedestal.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.000
“The Great Replacement is happening!

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:36.000
While you are hard at work, cuckoos from Africa are coming to your nests, kicking out your eggs to replace them by their own.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:49.000
And don’t think it’s only the cuckoos! Do the maths: we pigeons lay two eggs a year. A sand martin lays five eggs per season. Warblers up to nine eggs!

00:01:49.000 --> 00:02:00.000
I’m asking you, my friends. Do you really want to live in a city you don’t recognise anymore, that has been taken over by these parasites?

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:13.000
Migrants take our nests, our food, our heritage. In ten years, if we don’t do anything, there will be no pigeons. Is this what you want?”

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:16.000
Then he looked at me. They all looked at me.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:23.000
I froze. They started marching in my direction, but I could not move.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:28.000
Their voices surrounded me, muffled, distant like in a bad dream.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:35.000
And then, I heard someone whispering in my ear: it was a very old crow, and she looked angry.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:42.000
“What are you still doing there, you idiot? Fly, birdie, fly for your life.”

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:50.000
So I did. I flew as fast as I could.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.000
I could still hear them behind me, they were big birds, but fear makes you faster.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:06.000
I flew until my wings ached, going in circles over the city, desperately trying to spot a place to hide, to find a way out in that ocean of concrete.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:12.000
Finally, I landed in a dark little alleyway, exhausted.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:18.000
It smelled like garbage, but at least it was quiet and I was alone.
